  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In all of the procedures discussed above, the maternal effect was treated as a general feature of the mother, with no specific character in the mother being identified as contributing to the effect. An alternative approach is to identify explicitly one or more maternal characters that are likely to be the source of the maternal effect, and to consider how these modify the expression of other characters in offspring. Falconer (1965a) introduced a simple model in which a single maternal character affects its own expression, e.g., maternal body size influencing the size of offspring via maternal care effects. Under this model, an individual's phenotype is described in the usual way, with the addition of a third term describing the maternal effect,",
      "source_page": 718,
      "printed_page": 698,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter23:p020:b6",
        "Genetics_chapter23:p020:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        132.0,
        1055.0,
        1093.0,
        1375.0
      ],
