    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Here $ z_{i1} $ represents the phenotype of individual i's mother (the 1 denoting 1 generation back), and m, the maternal effect coefficient, is defined as the partial regression of offspring phenotype on maternal phenotype, holding the genetic contribution constant. (We will see shortly that m is just the difference between maternal-offspring and paternal-offspring regressions, as previously denoted). The remainder of the phenotype is assumed to be determined by an additive genetic effect $ A_{i} $ and an independently distributed residual deviation $ E_{i} $.",
      "source_page": 718,
      "printed_page": 698,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter23:p020:b10",
        "Genetics_chapter23:p021:b2"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        132.0,
        1439.0,
        1094.0,
        1540.0
      ],
      "source_page_end": 719,
      "source_pages": [
        718,
        719
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          132.0,
          1439.0,
          1094.0,
          1540.0
        ],
        [
          68.0,
          138.0,
          1029.0,
          265.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "631f9e35b75bd14c3440c7fb59669b18b757669698dd892c6b9efbc82c77a680"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "By extension, the phenotype of the mother can be written as a function of her mother, i.e., $ z_{i1} = A_{i1} + mz_{i2} + E_{i1} $, and that is the case for the phenotype $ z_{i2} $ of i's grandmother, as well as for all more remote members of i's maternal lineage. Consequently, the phenotype of i can be expressed by the infinite series,",
      "source_page": 719,
      "printed_page": 699,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter23:p021:b3"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        67.0,
